WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.418 --> 00:00:04.331
- Six foot seven, outta
Kansas City, Missouri!

00:00:04.331 --> 00:00:05.164
(players cheering)

00:00:05.164 --> 00:00:07.120
- [Narrator] This is BYU
men's basketball team,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.100
getting hyped for one of their first tests

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.480
in this year's NCAA tournament.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.999
A test for the coronavirus.

00:00:13.999 --> 00:00:15.510
(upbeat music)

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.603
March madness is now underway,

00:00:17.603 --> 00:00:18.436
- [Player] I'm back!

00:00:18.436 --> 00:00:19.350
- [Narrator] And COVID-19

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.000
is the opponent every team is facing.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.370
In the tournament's first weekend

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.110
an outbreak on one roster

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.410
forced a team to forfeit
before even taking the court.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.390
Others have competed without key players.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.510
- The winning team is going to be

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:35.210
that that can keep themselves
safe as possible, right?

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.360
- [Narrator] But safety
isn't just up to the teams.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.580
The NCAA has created so-called
controlled environments

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.720
in Indianapolis, where
the men are playing,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.110
and San Antonio, where the women are.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.610
- In order to pull this off,

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.540
the NCAA did what it does best,

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.110
which is write lots and lots of rules.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.220
- [Narrator] Last year,
the virus put March Madness

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:59.220
and the nearly $900 million
in revenue it brings on ice.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.000
So what are the protocols
the NCAA put in place

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.510
to keep COVID-19 from upending
this year's tournament?

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:06.912
And are they working?

00:01:06.912 --> 00:01:09.250
(players cheering)

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.970
- Last year we knew
nothing about this virus.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.160
Now we know a lot. Now we have testing.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.600
Last year, last year,

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.720
part of the reason we
canceled the tournament

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.100
is there wasn't enough testing available

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.740
to do the testing we needed to do.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.230
- [Narrator] Much of the
NCAA's COVID 19 playbook

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.680
centers around testing, quarantining,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.170
masking, and distancing.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.970
Just to enter the controlled environment,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.830
players and coaches were all required

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.000
to report seven consecutive
days of negative tests.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.340
- I'm in quarantine right now.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:37.770
- [Narrator] Then upon arrival,

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.080
everyone had to complete
a 48 hour quarantine.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.840
- We got tested about a hour ago.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.620
- They are getting tested every day.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.480
They don't have roommates
in their hotel rooms.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.450
Every floor of the hotel has its own team

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.730
and getting to practice

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.260
involves a very intricate dance of timing

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.200
of you know, who can be
in the elevator when,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.940
when they can walk across the lobby.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.167
Everything down to the
minutia is thought of.

00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:01.240
(player cheers)

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.010
- [Narrator] That's
because one negative test

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.280
has the potential to
cause a domino effect.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.360
- [Player] You know that's a bucket!

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.040
- [Narrator] Once a player tests positive,

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.030
any other player that is
deemed as a close contact

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.140
will also be required to quarantine,

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.020
potentially missing the
opportunity to play.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.030
- Some people will say, well, you know,

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:17.950
you're being too cautious.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:18.990
Absolutely.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:19.823
I think I'm being cautious

00:02:19.823 --> 00:02:23.330
because there's a lot of
things are at stake here.

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:24.370
- [Narrator] For the NCAA,

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.740
what's at stake is 80%
of its annual revenue,

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.340
which the association banks on

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.930
the men's basketball
tournament to bring in.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.220
- [Laine] When the NCAA's
revenue gets curtailed,

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.550
that means that all of the money

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.681
that it gives out to support
the athletic departments,

00:02:37.681 --> 00:02:40.390
every one of the thousand
member institutions

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:41.980
that make up the NCAA,

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.350
they also get hurt and
they get less money.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:45.183
- [Narrator] Last year,

00:02:45.183 --> 00:02:47.400
the NCAA's distribution to all schools

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.503
was projected to be $600 million.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:53.060
It ended up being just 225 million

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.930
after the tournament was canceled

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.300
- In order to avoid kind of an
existential financial crisis.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.150
the NCAA really made it a priority

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.240
to make sure that it could
hold the tournament this year

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.653
and continue to recoup
some of those revenues.

00:03:06.465 --> 00:03:08.410
(players cheering)

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.010
- [Narrator] For players and coaches,

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.903
what's at stake is the chance
to be national champions.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.070
A title VCU's men's team lost

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.420
before they even took the court.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.530
The team reported multiple positive tests

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:23.430
in the 48 hours proceeding
their first round game.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.440
VCU did not specify how many members

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.920
were deemed close contacts and
thus required to quarantine,

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.860
but teams need a minimum of
five healthy players to compete.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.580
- This is what you dream of as
a college player and a coach

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.170
and to get it taken away like this

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.110
is, it's just a heartbreaking moment

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:42.920
in, in their, in their young lives.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.130
- We knew that this was going to happen.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.810
There will be teams that you will find

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.160
with somebody infected

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.263
because there's enough virus
transmission in the community

00:03:49.263 --> 00:03:51.790
that that was going to be the case.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.502
And unfortunately, you
know, we are seeing that.

00:03:54.502 --> 00:03:57.170
- [Narrator] But the virus has
impacted teams in other ways.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.970
Oklahoma played without
its second leading scorer

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:01.680
and defending champions Virginia

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.160
lost their first round matchup

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:04.530
after forced quarantines

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.800
from an earlier outbreak
impacted their practice schedule.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.160
- I think you're going to be continuing

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:09.993
seeing this kind of things.

00:04:09.993 --> 00:04:11.590
And I think hopefully
teams are going to be

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.120
looking at their protocols

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.060
and what they're doing
and learning from this

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.880
and saying, could we
do something different?

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.130
- While Dr. Del Rio says

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.659
the NCAA's COVID protocols
are comprehensive,

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:24.310
its controlled environment
isn't impenetrable.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:26.467
- They have to find a way to, you know

00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:28.230
not run into trouble with this.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.910
And it's an incredibly
unpredictable and contagious virus.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:32.880
So it's sort of this X factor

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.431
that's hanging over every
team's tournament runs.

00:04:35.431 --> 00:04:38.348
(players cheering)

